\section{Discover the Call Graph from Value Flow}

In a first-order language the syntax of a call often identifies its callee. In a higher-order core, the operator might be a parameter, a value read from a record, or the result of another call. A simple closed-world analysis labels each lambda, and attaches a set of possible lambda labels to each expression result and binder. The sets represent a may property: a lambda appears if a corresponding closure could flow to that point. Initialize the sets to empty and seed each lambda expression with its own label. For an occurrence of a variable, include its binder's set in the occurrence's result set; for a \texttt{let}, include the right-hand-side set in the binder's set.

For a call \(p\,a\), every discovered lambda \(\ell\) in the set for \(p\) activates two constraints: flow the set for \(a\) into \(\ell\)'s formal, and flow the set for \(\ell\)'s body into the call's result. The appearance of new operator labels creates new call edges, which may generate more value flow in turn. A worklist stores the facts that changed and their dependent inclusion constraints. Since a finite program contains finitely many lambdas and each slot only grows, the iteration terminates. For records, an extension gives fields of abstract allocation sites their own sets; a projection joins the matching fields of all possible receiver sites.

\section{Worked Example: A Context Merge}

\begin{verbatim}
let pass = fun p -> p in
let inc  = fun n -> n + 1 in
let dbl  = fun n -> n + n in
let f = pass inc in
let g = pass dbl in
(f 10, g 10)
\end{verbatim}

A context-insensitive solution gives the formal \texttt{p} the set \(\{\mathit{inc},\mathit{dbl}\}\). Both calls to \texttt{pass} receive the same abstract result, so neither call in the final pair can be converted to an unconditional direct call on this information alone. In a one-call-site-sensitive variant, the two applications of \texttt{pass} get different abstract instances of its formal. Their singleton results identify \texttt{inc} and \texttt{dbl} separately, allowing direct calls and preserving the expected pair \((11,20)\). More context usually costs more memory and propagation. Truncating a call history to a bounded suffix merges instances, ensuring a finite domain but allowing imprecision to return along recursive paths.

Inserting a wrapper that calls \texttt{pass} at one textual site tests this limit: the two closures can merge again because the two dynamic calls through the wrapper have the same most recent site. An analysis may lose precision under this refactoring, but it must not lose any actual target.

\section{Use Sets as Evidence, Not Guesses}

The soundness invariant says that every concrete closure observed at an analyzed point has its creation label in the corresponding abstract set. A proof follows execution, using inclusions for lets, argument binding, and return. Test the invariant by instrumenting the evaluator with allocation labels and checking each observed call against the computed set, including calls through recursive results and captured variables. Passing tests does not prove that all possible calls were considered. In a separately compiled module, an imported higher-order value needs a conservative summary or an unknown target alternative. An empty set is not a justification to erase a call through foreign or dynamically supplied values. Even a singleton callee label says nothing by itself about the contents of different instances of its closure; a direct call must still supply the correct environment unless it can prove the environment unnecessary.

Suresh Jagannathan and Andrew Wright's \emph{Compiling Functional Languages with Flow Analysis} discusses how global information enables specialized call protocols and data representations. Such transformations also require facts beyond the label set, such as closure escape information for stack allocation or all relevant uses for unboxing. Neil D. Jones and Nils Andersen's \emph{Flow analysis of lazy higher-order functional programs} instead approximates values with regular sets of trees derived from a rewriting model. Its handling of lazy calling concerns call-by-name, not memoization; a result about reachable constructors is not a prediction of how many times an update runs. For importing optimization facts across builds, \emph{The Modern Compiler Dossier}, Chapter~38, proposes binding summaries to exact library and interface versions. A missing or mismatched summary must widen the analysis rather than quietly become a closed-world assumption.
